    {
      "id": 12,
      "type": "term",
      "text": "Explain FULL OUTER JOIN",
      "pages": [
        18,
        19
      ],
      "answer": "A **FULL OUTER JOIN** returns every row from both tables involved in the join.  \n- Rows that satisfy the join condition appear together (e.g., Alice – Laptop, Bob – Headphones, Carol – Smartphone).  \n- If a row from the left table has no matching row in the right table, the columns from the right table are filled with **NULL** (e.g., a customer without an order).  \n- Conversely, if a row from the right table has no matching row in the left table, the columns from the left table are filled with **NULL** (e.g., an order without a customer).  \n\nThus the result set includes all matches plus all non‑matches from both sides, with NULLs marking the missing counterpart values[1][4].",
      "citations": [
